WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.417
(soft music)

00:00:03.315 --> 00:00:05.280
- That just like hit me right here.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:08.130
- Mom, can you hear? I don't
want you to hear what it is.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:11.493
- This is heavy. How heavy do you get?

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:15.660
- Ah, yeah, so the most
hurtful thing I've been told,

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:18.363
'cause I've thought
about this for a while,

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:21.990
it wasn't told to me,
it was written to me.

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:25.200
My mom sent me an email, she
sent an email to everybody.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.740
So it doesn't feel like
it should hurt as much

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:30.630
if everybody got the
brunt end of the stick.

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:33.120
But she told all of us

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:36.153
that she no longer wants to be,

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:42.303
she no, sorry, I never shared like this.

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:49.680
She doesn't wanna be our mom anymore.

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:52.050
She's no longer anybody's mother,

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:53.913
she's no longer anybody's sister.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:59.130
She's just decided not
to be anybody's family.

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:04.130
So actually, when I
have spoken about this,

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:07.500
I'm usually a lot stronger
explaining what happened.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:12.500
So I don't know why I'm
crying at this point

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:15.840
when I don't usually cry when
I tell people about this.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:19.389
So, you know, life has been
super good after she left.

00:01:19.389 --> 00:01:23.310
I think it honestly brought
the family together,

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:27.136
a closer relationship, a
more deeper relationship

00:01:27.136 --> 00:01:30.660
with my aunties and my
siblings and my stepdad.

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:35.660
And so it, there's like a
silver lining to all of it.

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:41.120
- Recently my ex-boyfriend
called me on the phone one day

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:46.410
when we were still together
and told me that he hated me

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:51.410
and that I was a low-value
woman because of my past,

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.733
because of things that I've done.

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:59.850
And I never thought I could
be hurt by such simple words.

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:04.850
It caused me to change my
identity so drastically.

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:06.213
I shaved my head.

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:09.483
My hair's just finally growing out.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:13.710
From that hurt, I just
had to get away from it

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:17.469
and shed the person I was
and become someone different

00:02:17.469 --> 00:02:21.210
because that one thing hurt me so bad.

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:24.260
I just like lost myself in that pain.

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:28.020
And I'm finally starting
to come out of that pain

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:32.133
and coming into myself and
feeling my strength again.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:37.023
And I can still really feel
the hurt of those words.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:42.030
- I would say it's not
necessarily what I've been told,

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:44.790
but more around the actions.

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:46.680
So like when I was younger,

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:49.590
I used to have the worst
allergies ever, right?

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:51.450
And so, and I was also left-handed,

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:52.283
so it was like almost like this

00:02:52.283 --> 00:02:54.720
like awkward double whammy I was getting.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:56.580
And you know, like when
you're in like grade school

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:59.070
you guys would sit in
like these little cubes?

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:01.140
And so like sometimes my elbow would

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:04.800
like end up brushing up
against my classmate and

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:08.700
or I would end up sneezing and
slowly yet surely over time

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:10.590
it was like my desk was an island

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:14.190
and the rest of the the little
pod that I was supposed to be

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:17.100
was maybe like another
five feet away from me.

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:20.400
In the moment, I hated
life. I will admit that.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:24.420
But in retrospect, it was needed.

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:27.270
And 'cause at this point
like it's allowed me

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:28.830
to just be that much strong of a person

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:31.740
where it's like I know
that it's really not gonna

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:35.700
be that bad because this too shall pass.

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:37.590
I just need to keep pushing
my rock up the hill.

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:41.460
Sure it sucks today, but
eventually I picked my rock.

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:44.550
And for me, today, my
rock is playing guitar.

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:47.010
And so every day I'm
just playing more guitar,

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:49.054
trying to get better,
trying to find my sound.

00:03:49.054 --> 00:03:51.780
And if you could find that,

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:53.663
honestly, what more could you ask for?

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:57.000
- The time that the gynecologist told me

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:58.203
that I lost my child.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:02.280
I felt that literally like,

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:07.020
like if the cat was
ripping my heart out of me.

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:09.510
it was a disturbing feeling

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:11.340
that I never thought that I would felt.

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:13.680
And the most painful
thing about it is what?

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:15.300
That I was holding my dad's hand

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:19.323
and I saw my dad cry with
me for the first time.

00:04:21.628 --> 00:04:26.070
- Probably I don't
deserve such a nice kid.

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:30.480
I have two kids and somebody
told me that I don't deserve.

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:35.480
They are too good for me to be a father

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:42.177
because I have a quite a background

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:46.710
sometimes was a little shaky,

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:49.170
but this was the most hurtful.

00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:52.350
I felt sorry for this
person who said that.

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:54.723
Why would you say something like this?

00:04:56.100 --> 00:05:00.310
You know, it's like you have
to be very, very angry inside

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:03.300
to say something like this.

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:05.340
- [Interviewer] Do you still
carry that hurt with you?

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:07.720
Like does it still?
- No, no, no.

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:11.190
- [Interviewer] Okay.
- No, no, I don't.

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:16.190
Because it would destroy my peace of mind

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:20.703
and it would've destroyed me from inside.

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:23.700
- Repeat the question.

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:25.200
- [Interviewer] What's the most painful,

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:26.750
hurtful thing you've been told?

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:33.540
- All right, so I'm gonna share this

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:35.913
just because I think that I wanna,

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:39.090
I want people to love themselves

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:42.720
despite what they may have been told

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:45.420
that could have made them hate themselves.

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:50.420
I was told by my mother that
I should not have been born.

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:56.160
She repeated that to me
throughout my whole life.

00:05:57.930 --> 00:05:59.640
I don't remember how old I was

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:03.210
when she first told me that
she should have aborted me.

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:07.680
I love my life so much
that I don't understand

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:10.350
how anybody else could not love me.

00:06:10.350 --> 00:06:13.140
But I also don't know if it's
because she doesn't love me

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:15.960
or because she didn't want to be a mother.

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:17.280
I don't think it's the truth talking,

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:18.540
I think it's just her pain.

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:21.900
And I've never said it
like this out loud before.

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:24.753
So no one has ever asked me this question.

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:30.723
- It would be a while
back, like 10 years ago.

00:06:31.661 --> 00:06:33.753
It was actually my first girlfriend ever.

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:40.297
We were outside my house and she had said,

00:06:40.297 --> 00:06:42.600
"You're not enough for me."

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:47.283
And that kind of, kind of
hurt, you know, broke me.

00:06:48.146 --> 00:06:50.700
I was like, what? Like 15, 16.

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:54.363
And she told me that,
just completely broke me.

00:06:55.487 --> 00:06:58.530
Not gonna lie, every once
in a while, I'm in my bed,

00:06:58.530 --> 00:07:00.060
thinking about, hurts again.

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:03.213
Makes me think maybe I'm
still not enough for someone.

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:06.930
- [Interviewer] Have you
had a relationship since?

00:07:06.930 --> 00:07:08.820
- I have not, no.

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:10.170
I just gotta learn to love myself

00:07:10.170 --> 00:07:12.930
before I could love others.

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:14.130
But I'll think about it.

00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:17.520
- The thing at this time
that's coming to mind

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:20.610
is a time when I was a
freshman in high school.

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:25.260
I was, there was, my high school
was about 45 minutes away.

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:27.450
And so I was in the car with a bunch

00:07:27.450 --> 00:07:29.931
of other boys from where I lived

00:07:29.931 --> 00:07:32.550
and I was picked on a lot
'cause I was a lot smaller,

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:33.840
all the other kids knew each other.

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:36.469
I was the new kid. I was always
forced inside the middle.

00:07:36.469 --> 00:07:38.460
You know, they played punching games.

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:39.930
Anyways, at the end of the car ride

00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:42.540
I was crying this one time,
and one of the other guys

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:44.557
who I thought was like,
you know, my friend said,

00:07:44.557 --> 00:07:46.170
"Hey, I'm really sorry, like, you know,

00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:48.420
like I promise, like I
won't do that again."

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:50.010
Two days later, he was
up to the same thing

00:07:50.010 --> 00:07:52.493
and it really just, I don't
know, struck me really hard.

00:07:55.980 --> 00:07:58.107
And so yeah, you know,

00:07:58.107 --> 00:08:02.010
that was a very tough time in my life and,

00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:05.160
but I look back on that time,

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:08.450
being able to experience stuff
like that and overcome it,

00:08:08.450 --> 00:08:10.698
it teaches a lot of
resilience within a person.

00:08:10.698 --> 00:08:13.680
In adult life, you need
a lot of resilience,

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:16.686
you know, to really make
a impact on this world.

00:08:16.686 --> 00:08:20.010
And I feel like that's
something I really wanna do.

00:08:20.010 --> 00:08:23.160
- Oh God, I feel like I do know.

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:25.140
It's like not even something
somebody said to me,.

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:26.850
People just like,
there's been a few people

00:08:26.850 --> 00:08:29.550
who've asked like what's wrong with me?

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:32.550
And like if I'm on drugs before.

00:08:32.550 --> 00:08:34.500
There was like one
specific time at a party,

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:36.090
somebody was like, "What are you on?"

00:08:36.090 --> 00:08:37.710
And like would not give it up.

00:08:37.710 --> 00:08:39.480
They just would not stop asking.

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:41.610
Like I was drinking
'cause it was like a party

00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:43.200
but like barely.

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:44.640
And I just was like being normal

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:46.530
and they were like, "What are you on?

00:08:46.530 --> 00:08:48.450
Like no, tell me. Like seriously."

00:08:48.450 --> 00:08:52.440
And I was just like,
"I'm just, just normal.

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:54.180
Just like being myself."

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:57.570
I don't know, really makes
makes you feel like an outcast

00:08:57.570 --> 00:08:59.850
when people literally ask
like what's wrong with you

00:08:59.850 --> 00:09:01.710
and when you think you're just normal.

00:09:01.710 --> 00:09:04.350
I think it's also like what
people like about me too.

00:09:04.350 --> 00:09:08.250
So it's like I've learned to
like love that about myself

00:09:08.250 --> 00:09:10.110
and not like hate myself for it,

00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:15.110
which has been very like
good for me I guess, yeah.

00:09:16.290 --> 00:09:19.740
- My dad has told me he's
really disappointed in me

00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:20.940
when I was really young.

00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:23.550
And that kind of hurt, you know,

00:09:23.550 --> 00:09:25.830
hearing that you disappointed your father

00:09:25.830 --> 00:09:27.543
at 14 kind of hurts.

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:30.217
He just looked me up and down and said,

00:09:30.217 --> 00:09:32.220
"I'm really disappointed in you."

00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:36.600
And I just went back
inside and I went to bed

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:38.333
'cause that was kind of rough for me.

00:09:39.413 --> 00:09:41.250
- Ah, most hurtful thing.

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:43.350
I do remember very vividly

00:09:43.350 --> 00:09:46.440
when I was probably about 14 or 15,

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:48.360
walking home from school one day

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:53.360
and running into a
girlfriend and her boyfriend

00:09:53.370 --> 00:09:57.057
as I, as we walked by, he
stopped to talk to me, you know?

00:09:57.057 --> 00:09:59.340
And I kind of thought, "Oh, you
know, I'm being acknowledged

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:01.500
or something, like kind of a big deal."

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:03.217
But then he said,

00:10:03.217 --> 00:10:05.817
"Just wanted to know,
how did you get so ugly?"

00:10:06.660 --> 00:10:08.580
And it was just something that did

00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:10.500
actually stick with me over the years.

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:15.500
It was defining in a way that it made me,

00:10:15.870 --> 00:10:17.850
you know, it sort of contributed who I am.

00:10:17.850 --> 00:10:19.710
I've always been sort of one

00:10:19.710 --> 00:10:23.460
to make a little bit of extra effort,

00:10:23.460 --> 00:10:26.580
although you can't tell
now but I'm like old.

00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:29.943
But that's just what popped into my head.

00:10:30.870 --> 00:10:34.170
- I was a kid boarding school.
I was in the seventh grade.

00:10:34.170 --> 00:10:37.620
The seventh grade teacher
was the principal.

00:10:37.620 --> 00:10:41.107
And we were in class and he said to me,

00:10:41.107 --> 00:10:44.190
"Michael, there's no
conceit in your family."

00:10:44.190 --> 00:10:45.270
And I'm going, "Oh, whoa."

00:10:45.270 --> 00:10:46.720
He says, "You've got it all."

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:49.983
I didn't know I was conceited.

00:10:51.210 --> 00:10:52.860
He told me I was.

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:57.420
And so that's when I started
questioning that part of me.

00:10:57.420 --> 00:11:01.680
I was still developing and
I was in a boarding school

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:06.210
and I had a bad, my background

00:11:08.250 --> 00:11:10.920
was tumultuous, to say the least.

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:11.970
When I was five years old,

00:11:11.970 --> 00:11:15.240
my mother's second husband molested me.

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:17.173
I blurted it out to my uncle that,

00:11:17.173 --> 00:11:19.320
what my father made me do.

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:21.482
They called my mother.
My mother divorced him.

00:11:21.482 --> 00:11:23.760
Several years later,
she married another man,

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:26.002
he molested me. I was in the third grade.

00:11:26.002 --> 00:11:29.847
And so that's when I got
taken out of my family

00:11:29.847 --> 00:11:32.160
or away from my mother,
and put in boarding school

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:35.490
because my grandmother
knew that was going down

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:36.323
and she didn't know it.

00:11:36.323 --> 00:11:39.303
My mother was an alcoholic,
her husbands were alcoholics.

00:11:40.170 --> 00:11:42.723
She was indifferent to me, generally.

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:45.630
I was the product of her first marriage

00:11:45.630 --> 00:11:48.360
being dragged along in her second marriage

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:49.320
and her third marriage.

00:11:49.320 --> 00:11:50.400
And I don't think she was happy

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:54.000
because I was the cause of the breakup

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:54.833
of her second marriage.

00:11:54.833 --> 00:11:57.180
And I think she really
was in love with this guy.

00:11:57.180 --> 00:11:58.530
She had three kids by him.

00:11:58.530 --> 00:12:01.773
After I revealed what he was
doing and they got divorced,

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:05.070
I think that really turned
her off to me in general.

00:12:05.070 --> 00:12:06.930
Like, "I just don't like this kid."

00:12:06.930 --> 00:12:08.880
So boarding school was
a good place for me.

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:11.280
I went into boarding school,
came out of boarding school,

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:12.750
went to high school for one year.

00:12:12.750 --> 00:12:13.890
So I joined the Navy.

00:12:13.890 --> 00:12:18.570
After 10 years, and here's
another moment, I'm gay.

00:12:18.570 --> 00:12:22.650
In the 10th year of my
enlistment, I got outed

00:12:22.650 --> 00:12:25.920
and the Office of Naval Intelligence,

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:28.080
ONI, came after me, threatened me

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:30.690
and I didn't want to
get kicked out, but I.

00:12:30.690 --> 00:12:33.217
They had me, I couldn't
reenlist, so I just said,

00:12:33.217 --> 00:12:34.740
"Okay, you won't see me around

00:12:34.740 --> 00:12:37.020
when my enlistment is up in three months."

00:12:37.020 --> 00:12:39.780
And I did my GED, I got out,

00:12:39.780 --> 00:12:41.400
got my bachelor's degree in nursing

00:12:41.400 --> 00:12:43.770
and then about 10 years ago, I retired.

00:12:43.770 --> 00:12:47.913
So I am now an RN. Registered
nurse? No, retired nurse.

00:12:49.020 --> 00:12:50.040
- [Interviewer] You've been through a lot.

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:52.410
How were you able to like push through it?

00:12:52.410 --> 00:12:53.243
- I don't know, you know,

00:12:53.243 --> 00:12:55.260
I've often asked myself that question.

00:12:55.260 --> 00:12:57.810
I was a, I was running scared,

00:12:57.810 --> 00:13:00.300
but I was determined not to fall.

00:13:00.300 --> 00:13:03.540
See, I've got a lot of
blessings in my life.

00:13:03.540 --> 00:13:04.800
I'm 82 years old.

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:06.690
I have no medical problems.

00:13:06.690 --> 00:13:08.880
I have no financial problems.

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:09.870
Which is all just to say,

00:13:09.870 --> 00:13:12.450
what a awesome life I live, you know?

00:13:13.290 --> 00:13:15.750
- I think the most painful
thing I was ever told

00:13:15.750 --> 00:13:18.180
was when I was five years old,

00:13:18.180 --> 00:13:20.357
my aunt pulled me aside

00:13:20.357 --> 00:13:23.490
and she told me I was never
going to amount to anything,

00:13:23.490 --> 00:13:26.520
that I was going to be a hoe.

00:13:26.520 --> 00:13:30.690
I was gonna have multiple
men. I was gonna be a failure.

00:13:30.690 --> 00:13:35.013
I wasn't gonna do anything
that meant anything.

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:36.810
And that really stuck with me

00:13:36.810 --> 00:13:37.950
for a really, really long time.

00:13:37.950 --> 00:13:40.680
I think it was part of the reason

00:13:40.680 --> 00:13:43.135
why I try so hard to make change now,

00:13:43.135 --> 00:13:45.480
because I'm trying to prove her wrong.

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:47.850
I don't hate anyone, but, she, she,

00:13:47.850 --> 00:13:50.820
I'm like this close to hating this woman.

00:13:50.820 --> 00:13:52.890
But I also feel like I
kind of want to thank her

00:13:52.890 --> 00:13:56.610
for being so evil, because
now I only see the world

00:13:56.610 --> 00:13:58.230
with kindness and I try to make sure

00:13:58.230 --> 00:14:01.440
like I exemplify that in
everything in my life.

00:14:01.440 --> 00:14:04.020
And I feel like with time,
I've like developed myself

00:14:04.020 --> 00:14:08.040
and I'm able to finally like
give people that kindness

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:09.940
that I wish I had when I was five, so.

00:14:10.841 --> 00:14:13.258
(soft music)

